.TH CLIENTAGE 8 "November 2026" "clientage 0.1.0" "Xymon plugins"
.SH NAME
clientage \- the age of a Xymon client's last message, as a column of its host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./xymon-plugin install --client clientage
.br
.B ./xymon-plugin enable --client clientage
.SH DESCRIPTION
On a monitored host -- a standalone Xymon client, or a Xymon server's own
client tree, the server named:
.B "XYMONHOME=<server> ./xymon-plugin install --client clientage"
-- its task, off until
.BR "enable --client" ,
runs every 5 minutes and sends the host's status
.BR clientage :
green while the Xymon client's last message is younger than 15 minutes,
yellow up to 30, red beyond; yellow when there is no message yet, as just
after the client starts. A client whose launcher still runs but whose
.I xymonclient.sh
hangs or fails is seen here before its columns go purple.
.PP
The age is read from the clock the Xymon client writes in its message,
.IR $XYMONTMP/msg.MACHINEDOTS.txt ,
the
.B [clock]
section's epoch, against
.B "logfetch --clock"
now: the same on every system Xymon runs on.
.PP
The two thresholds, in minutes, are the arguments of its task's
.BR CMD ,
in
.IR ext/clientage/tasks.cfg ;
an update keeps that file as edited.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XYMONHOME/ext/clientage/
The client part: the script and its task file.
.TP
.I $XYMONCLIENTLOGS/clientage-client.log
Its log, one line a run; on a server's own client tree, in the server's logs.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xymon-plugin (8),
.BR xymonclient.cfg (5),
.BR logfetch (1)
